# Not the [[same]]; [[different]].
#: {{ux|en|Do you know '''another''' way to do this job?}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=22|passage=From '''another''' point of view, it was a place without a soul. The well-to-do had hearts of stone; the rich were brutally bumptious; the Press, the Municipality, all the public men, were ridiculously, vaingloriously self-satisfied.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=w:Michael Ende|tlr=w:Ralph Manheim|title=w:The Neverending Story|page=53|isbn=0140386335|passage=But that is '''''another''''' story and will be told another time.}}
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|year=2013|month=May-June|author=[http://www.americanscientist.org/authors/detail/katrina-g-claw Katrina G. Claw]|title=Rapid Evolution in Eggs and Sperm|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/rapid-evolution-in-eggs-and-sperm|volume=101|issue=3|magazine=w:American Scientist|passage=In plants, the ability to recognize self from nonself plays an important role in fertilization, because self-fertilization will result in less diverse offspring than fertilization with pollen from '''another''' individual.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170609053207/http://www.americanscientist.org/issues/feature/2013/3/rapid-evolution-in-eggs-and-sperm|archivedate=9 June 2017}}
# [[any|Any]] or [[some]] other, similar in likeness or in effect, instead.
#: {{ux|en|One gold ingot is valued the same as '''another''', but gemstones are valued individually.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1983|author=w:Michael Ende|tlr=w:Ralph Manheim|title=w:The Neverending Story|page=53|isbn=0140386335|passage=But that is another story and will be told '''''another''''' time.}}

====Usage notes====
* As a fused head construction ''another'' may have a possessive ''another's'' (plural: ''others'' or possessive ''others'''). It is much used in opposition to ''one''; as, "one went one way, another went another". It is also used with ''one'' in a reciprocal sense; as, "love [[one another]]," that is, let each love the other or others.
** [[w:en:John Milton|John Milton]]
**: {{ux|en|These two imparadised in one '''another's''' arms.}}
* ''Another'' is usually used with a singular noun, but constructions such as "another five days", "another twenty miles", "another few people", "another fifty dollars" are valid too.
* Sometimes, the word ''whole'' is inserted into ''another'' by the common process of [[tmesis]], giving: "a whole nother." This is a colloquialism that some recommend avoiding in formal writing.<ref>{{cite-web|url=https://brians.wsu.edu/2016/05/19/a-whole-nother/ |title=a whole ’nother. Common Errors in English Usage and More|last=Brians|first=Paul|date=2016-05-19|publisher=[[w:Washington State University|Washington State University]]|accessdate=2019-12-30|passage=It is one thing to use the expression “a whole ’nother” as a consciously slangy phrase suggesting rustic charm and a completely different matter to use it mistakenly.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260417060626/https://brians.wsu.edu/2016/05/19/a-whole-nother/|archivedate=17 April 2026}}</ref> The prescribed alternatives are "a whole other" or "another whole".
* There may be ambiguity: ''another'' may or may not imply replacement, e.g. "I need another chair." may mean "My chair needs to be replaced." or "I need an additional chair [and I need to keep my existing chair]."

