===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Someone who watches over something; a person in charge of something or someone.
#: {{ux|en|The camp '''monitors''' look after the children during the night, when the teachers are asleep.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1829|author=Charles Sprague|title=s:To My Cigar
|passage=And oft, mild friend, to me thou art<br>A '''monitor''', though still;<br>Thou speak'st a lesson to my heart,<br>Beyond the preacher's skill.}}
# A device that detects and informs on the presence, quantity, etc., of something.
# {{senseid|en|Q5290}} {{lb|en|computing}} A device similar to a [[television set]] used as to give a graphical display of the output from a [[computer]].
#: {{ux|en|The information flashed up on the '''monitor'''.}}
# A [[studio monitor]] or [[loudspeaker]].
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[program]] for viewing and editing.
#: {{ux|en|a machine code '''monitor'''}}
# {{lb|en|computing|dated}} The [[command line interface]] of an [[operating system]].
# {{lb|en|Hong Kong|Singapore|archaic|_|in|_|British}} A student leader in a class.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1871|author=w:Henry William Pullen| title=s:The Fight at Dame Europa's School| passage=So, as she did not like the masters to be prying about the play-ground out of school, she chose from among the biggest and most trustworthy of her pupils five '''monitors''', who had authority over the rest of the Boys, and kept the unruly ones in order.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1881|author=w:Talbot Baines Reed| title=[[s:The Fifth Form at St. Dominic's/Chapter X|The Fifth Form at St. Dominic's]]| chapter=X| passage=But it was not so—at least, not always—for though they fell out among themselves, they united their forces against the common enemy—the '''monitors'''!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=|author=Pearl Poon|url=https://books.google.com.hk/books?id=Seg7DQAAQBAJ&pg=PP8|passage=He learned that a '''monitor''' should assist the teachers in distributing worksheets, maintaining class discipline, helping classmates in need and so on.|title=Class Monitor Election|publisher={{w|Hong Kong ICAC}} Comics}}
# {{senseid|en|Q137823}} {{lb|en|nautical}} A relatively small [[armored]] [[warship]] with only one or two [[turret]]s (but often carrying unusually large [[gun]]s for a warship of its size), usually designed for [[shore]] [[bombardment]] or [[riverine]] [[warfare]] rather than [[open]]-[[ocean]] [[combat]].  {{defdt|from 1862}}
# A [[monitor lizard]] ({{taxfmt|Varanus|genus}} spp. and extinct relatives in family {{taxfmt|Varanidae|family}}).
# {{lb|en|engineering}} A tool holder, as for a lathe, shaped like a low turret, and capable of being revolved on a vertical pivot so as to bring several tools successively into position.
# A [[monitor nozzle]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} One who admonishes; one who warns of faults, informs of duty, or gives advice and instruction by way of reproof or caution.
#* '''c. 1620''', {{w|Francis Bacon}}, letter of advice to Sir {{w|George Villiers, 1st Duke of Buckingham|George Villiers}}
#*: You need not be a '''monitor''' to your gracious master the king.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1873|title=Gardeners Chronicle & New Horticulturist|page=119|passage=There has been no lack of other '''monitors''' — a ticklish [[haysel]], a flooded harvest all through the north {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} An [[ironclad]] warship with a low [[freeboard]] and a rotating turret.
# A modern shallow-draft warship for coastal bombardment.

