From {{inh|en|enm|chacen}}, from {{der|en|xno|chacer}}, {{der|en|fro|chacier}}, from {{der|en|la-vul|*captiāre}}, from {{der|en|la|captō|captāre}}, frequentative of {{m|la|capiō|capere}}. Compare {{cog|fr|chasser||to hunt”, “to chase}}, {{cog|es|cazar||to hunt}}, {{cog|pt|caçar||to hunt}}
, see {{cog|no|skysse||to hunt}}.
{{doublet|en|catch}} and related to {{m|en|capture}}. Displaced native {{cog|ang|ōht}}, {{m|ang|ēhtnes}}, and {{m|ang|wāþ}}. Broadly overtook {{cog|ang|huntaþ}}.

{{s|to nag; to pressure}} Likely influenced by {{m|yue|催||to urge; to pressure}} and {{m|yue|追||to chase}}, which are near-homophones.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|chace}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of one who chases another; a [[pursuit]].
# A [[hunt]]; the act of hunting; the pursuit of game.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1828|author=w:Thomas Keightley|title=The Fairy Mythology|volume=II|location=London|publisher=William Harrison Ainsworth|page=58|passage=He was a good shot and passionately fond of the '''chase''', and used to spend many hours in the neighbouring woods after the harts and the wild sows.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1861|author={{w|Elizabeth Gaskell}}|title=The Grey Woman
|passage=By-and-by, she wandered away to an unnecessary revelation of her master's whereabouts: gone to help in the search for his landlord, the Sieur de Poissy, who lived at the château just above, and who had not returned from his '''chase''' the day before; so the intendant imagined he might have met with some accident, and had summoned the neighbours to beat the forest and the hill-side.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1981|author=w:William Irwin Thompson|title=The Time Falling Bodies Take to Light: Mythology, Sexuality and the Origins of Culture|publisher=Rider/Hutchinson & Co.|location=London|page=134|passage=Through male bonding, the subculture of the hunt caught up in the mystique of the '''chase''', the hunting party became a military force, and men discovered that they need not stop at defense: they could go out to hunt for other people's wealth.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} A children's game where one player chases another.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Marla Pender McGhee|title=Quick & Fun Learning Activities for 1 Year Olds| page=25| passage=Some children like to be caught when playing '''chase''', and others do not.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Martin J. Levin|title=We Were Relentless: A Family's Journey to Overcome Disability| page=41| passage=So we played '''chase''' up and down the concourses of the airport.}}
# {{lb|en|British}} A large [[country]] [[estate]] where [[game]] may be [[shoot|shot]] or [[hunt]]ed.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Bleak House|14|text=Outside, the stately oaks, rooted for ages in the green ground which has never known ploughshare, but was still a '''chase''' when kings rode to battle with sword and shield and rode a-hunting with bow and arrow, bear witness to his greatness.}}
# {{lb|en|UK|in place names}} An area of unenclosed land formerly reserved for hunting, such as [[Cannock Chase]].
# Anything being chased, especially a vessel in time of war.
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[wild]] [[animal]] that is [[hunt]]ed.
#: {{syn|en|game}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1575|author=w:George Gascoigne|title=The Noble Arte of Venerie of Hunting|location=London|publisher=Christopher Barker|chapter=40|page=111|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A14021.0001.001
|passage=As touching the [[hart|Harte]] and such other light '''chases''' or beasts of [[venery|Uenerie]], the huntesmen on horsebacke may followe theyr houndes alwayes by the same wayes that they saw him passe ouer,}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-2|V|2|text=Hold, Warwick, seek thee out some other '''chase''',<br>For I myself must hunt this deer to death.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} Any of the [[gun]]s that fire directly ahead or astern; either a [[bow chase]] or [[stern chase]].
# {{lb|en|[[real tennis]]}} The occurrence of a second [[bounce]] by the [[ball]] in certain areas of the [[court]], giving the [[server]] the chance, later in the game, to "play off" the chase from the receiving end and possibly win the [[point]].
# {{lb|en|[[real tennis]]}} A division of the floor of a gallery, marked by a figure or otherwise; the spot where a ball falls, and between which and the [[dedans]] the adversary must drive the ball in order to gain a point.
# {{lb|en|cycling}} One or more riders who are ahead of the [[peloton]] and trying to join the race or stage [[leader]]s.
# {{lb|en|music}} A series of brief [[improvise]]d [[jazz]] [[solo]]s by a number of [[musician]]s taking turns.

