# {{cap|ought}} to; {{n-g|indicating opinion, advice, or instruction, about what is required or desirable.}}
## {{n-g|Used to issue an instruction (traditionally seen as carrying less force of authority than alternatives such as 'shall' or 'must').}}
##: {{ux|en|You '''should''' never drink and drive.}}
##: {{ux|en|The law is clear that you '''should''' always wear a seat belt.}}
##: {{ux|en|The manual says that this switch '''should''' be in the 'off' position.}}
## {{n-g|Used to give advice or opinion that an action is, or would have been, beneficial or desirable.}}
##: {{ux|en|You '''should''' go and see that film. I think you'll enjoy it.}}
##: {{ux|en|'''Should''' we go and see the police, do you think?}}
##: {{ux|en|She '''should''' not have been so rude.}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} {{n-g|With verbs such as 'see' or 'hear', usually in the second person, used to point out something remarkable in either a good or bad way.}}
##: ''You '''should''' see his new apartment. It's like a palace!''
##: ''If you think her piano playing is bad, you '''should''' hear her sing!''
## {{n-g|In questions, asks what is correct, proper, desirable, etc.}}
##: {{ux|en|What do you think? What '''should''' I do?}}
##* {{RQ:Guardian
|date=21 August 2012
|author=Ed Pilkington
|title=Death penalty on trial: should Reggie Clemons live or die?
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/world/2012/aug/21/death-penalty-trial-reggie-clemons?newsfeed=true
|passage=Next month, Clemons will be brought before a court presided over by a "special master", who will review the case one last time. The hearing will be unprecedented in its remit, but at its core will be a simple issue: '''should''' Reggie Clemons live or die?
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261015080946/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/aug/21/death-penalty-trial-reggie-clemons?newsfeed=true
|archivedate=15 October 2026
}}
# {{cap|ought}} to; {{n-g|expressing expectation.}}
## {{n-g|Indicates that something is expected to have happened or to be the case now.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''should''' have finished by now; I'll call them to check.}}
##: {{ux|en|My fruit trees '''should''' be in flower, but the cold spring has set them back.}}
## Will be [[likely]] to (become or do something); {{non-gloss|indicates a degree of possibility or probability that the stated thing will happen or be true in the future.}}
##: {{ux|en|They '''should''' have it finished by Friday.}}
##: {{ux|en|When you press this button, the pilot flame '''should''' ignite.}}
##: {{ux|en|You '''should''' be warm enough with that coat.}}
##: {{ux|en|We’re spending the winter in Ibiza. ~ That '''should''' be nice.}}
# {{senseid|en|auxiliary for present subjunctive}} {{lb|en|subjunctive}} {{non-gloss|Used to form a variant of the present [[subjunctive mood|subjunctive]], expressing a state or action that is hypothetical, potential, mandated, etc.}}
#: {{syn|en|were|alt=were to|id=subjunctive}}
#: {{ux|en|If I '''should''' be late, I '''should''' hope that you are prepared to go on without me.}}
#: {{ux|en|If he '''should''' have seen her, he'll have given him my message.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Should''' you need extra blankets, you will find them in the closet.}}
#: {{ux|en|It's his proposal that there ('''should''') not be several dozen units.}}
#: {{ux|en|I regret that it '''should''' have happened, but I was really shocked that he '''should''' say that.}}
#* {{RQ:Noyes Poems|poem=The Highwayman|stanza=V|page=47|passage='One kiss, my bonny sweetheart, I'm after a prize to-night, / But I shall be back with the yellow gold before the morning light; / Yet, if they press me sharply, and harry me through the day, / Then look for me by moonlight, / Watch for me by moonlight, / I'll come to thee by moonlight, though Hell '''should''' bar the way.'}}
#* {{RQ:Williams Velveteen Rabbit|passage=It was a long weary time, for the Boy was too ill to play, and the little Rabbit found it rather dull with nothing to do all day long. But he snuggled down patiently, and looked forward to the time when the Boy '''should''' be well again, and they would go out in the garden amongst the flowers and the butterflies and play splendid games in the raspberry thicket like they used to.}}
#* {{RQ:Wonder Fool
|passage=And I'd be a fool right now<br>If I '''should''' hurt you girl<br>And I'd be a fool right now<br>If I '''should''' leave you girl}}
#* '''2008''', Peter Michael Higgins, ''Number Story: From Counting to Cryptography'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=HcIwkWXy3CwC&pg=PA141&dq=%22should+he+be+right%22&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0CEYQ6AEwB2oVChMIpKTWksbhyAIVU9ljCh1O2AAe#v=onepage&q=%22should%20he%20be%20right%22&f=false page 141 (Google Books view)]:
#*: He is noted for coming up with his 'wager', in which he argued that he was prepared to believe in God on the grounds that he had nothing to lose if he was wrong, and everything to gain '''should''' he be right.
# {{senseid|en|would}}{{n-g|An alternative to ''[[would]]'' with first person subjects.}}
## {{lb|en|formal|_|or|_|literary}} {{n-g|Used to express a conditional outcome.}}
##: {{ux|en|If I had not been so tired, I '''should''' have laughed heartily.}}
##* {{RQ:Baum Wizard of Oz|passage="If our friends, the Scarecrow and the Tin Woodman, were only with us," said the Lion, "I '''should''' be quite happy."}}
##* {{RQ:Baum Wizard of Oz|passage="Your Silver Shoes will carry you over the desert," replied Glinda. "If you had known their power you could have gone back to your Aunt Em the very first day you came to this country." "But then I '''should''' not have had my wonderful brains!" cried the Scarecrow. "I might have passed my whole life in the farmer's cornfield."|23}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1950|author=Norman Lindsay|authorlink=Norman Lindsay|title={{w|Dust or Polish?}}|publisher=Angus and Robertson|location=Sydney|page=152|passage="Good God! I don't want another accident here. I '''should''' go dotty if I had to face all that again."}}
## {{lb|en|formal|_|or|_|literary|_|outside certain combinations such as with 'imagine' or 'think'}} {{n-g|Used to impart a tentative, conjectural or polite nuance.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''should''' imagine that they have arrived by now.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''should''' think you would apologize.}}
##: {{ux|en|I '''should''' be very grateful to receive your prompt reply. {{q|formal or old-fashioned}}}}
##: {{ux|en|We '''should''' very much like to meet her. {{q|formal or old-fashioned}}}}
##: {{ux|en|It didn’t turn out as we intended it '''should'''.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1817|author=Jane Austen|title=Northanger Abbey|passage=I '''should''' like to dine with him. I dare say he gives famous dinners.}}
## {{n-g|Used to express what the speaker would do in another person's situation, as a means of giving a suggestion or recommendation.}}
##: ''It's disgraceful the way that they've treated you. I '''should''' write and complain.''
##* {{RQ:Bramah Bravo of London|passage=As this happens to be Saturday it gives you a day's grace. I '''should''' go round and see the chiefs to-morrow. Tell them all about it and what I have said.}}

====Usage notes====
* ''Should'' has, as its most common meaning in modern English, the sense ''ought'' as in ''I should go, but I don't see how I can''. However, the older sense as the subjunctive of the future indicative auxiliary, ''shall'', is often used with ''I'' or ''we'' to indicate a more polite form than ''would'': ''I should like to go, but I can't; Were he to arrive, I should be pleased''. In much speech and writing, ''should'' has been replaced by ''would'' in contexts of this kind, but it remains in conditional subjunctives: ''should'' (never ''would'') ''I go, I should wear my new dress; should he remain, he would be granted asylum.''
* {{sense|obligation}} Contrast with stronger auxiliary verb {{m|en|must}}, which indicates that the speaker believes the subject is [[require]]d to execute the predicate, or [[have to]] which indicates that the speaker believes the subject is required to execute, although speaker might disagree with the principle, and [[should]] which is merely [[advice]] – take it or leave it.
* {{sense|likely}} Possibility, or probability. Contrast with stronger auxiliary verb in the affirmative {{m|en|must}}, and negative sense {{m|en|can't}}, which indicate that there is a logical imperative [[certainty]] that the subject will (or will not) execute the predicate. Also compare with the weaker {{m|en|might}}, which indicates at most a 50/50 possibility, or probability.
* {{sense|subjunctive}} In American English, the present subjunctive is commonly used instead of ''should'' (e.g., "suggest that he ''stay''"), while in British English, ''should'' is commoner (e.g., "suggest that he ''should'' stay"). Both forms of English, however, sometimes use ''should'' in certain conditionals (e.g., "If I ''should'' be in trouble, I shall call you"). Furthermore, ''should'' is not used in independent clauses with the present subjunctive, many of which clauses are now fossilized expressions (e.g., "Peace ''be'' with you", "''suffice'' it to say"; never ''should be'' or ''should suffice'').
* See the usage notes at {{m|en|shall}}.

