===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|reule}}, {{m|enm|rewle}}, {{m|enm|rule}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|riule}}, {{m|fro|reule}}, from {{der|en|la|regula||straight stick, bar, ruler, pattern}}, from {{m|la|regō||to keep straight, direct, govern, rule}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₃réǵeti||to straighten; right}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}; see {{m|en|regent}}. {{doublet|en|rail|regal#Noun|regula|rigol}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[regulation]], [[law]], [[guideline]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:rule}}
#: {{ux|en|All participants must adhere to the '''rules'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|You have to follow the '''rules''' to enter the qualifiers for the football tournament.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1694|author=w:John Tillotson|title=Of The Obligations of Christians to a Holy Life
|passage=We profess to have embraced a religion which contains the most exact '''rules''' for the government of our lives.}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=68| title=T time|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579860-g8-pledges-tackle-three-ts-t-time| passage=The ability to shift profits to low-tax countries by locating intellectual property in them{{...}}is often assumed to be the preserve of high-tech companies.{{...}}current tax '''rules''' make it easy for all sorts of firms to generate{{...}}“stateless income”: profit subject to tax in a jurisdiction that is neither the location of the factors of production that generate the income nor where the parent firm is domiciled.}}
# A regulating principle.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare All's Well|1|1|passage=There's little can be said in 't; 'Tis against the '''rule''' of nature.}}
