===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|thinken}}, {{m|enm|thynken}}, {{m|enm|thenken}} (also {{m|enm|thinchen}}, {{m|enm|thunchen|thünchen}}), from {{inh|en|ang|þyncan||to seem, appear}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*þunkijaną||to seem}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|dunken||to seem, appear}}, {{cog|de|dünken||to seem, appear}}, {{cog|da|tykkes||to seem}}, {{cog|sv|tycka||to seem, think, regard}}, {{cog|is|þykja||to be regarded, be considered, seem}}. More at {{l|en|methinks}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|~s|~ing|thought}}

# {{senseid|en|seem}} {{lb|en|obsolete|_|except in ''[[methinks]]''}} To [[seem]], to [[appear]].
#* {{RQ:Mlry MrtDrthr|17.5|2|XV|v|passage=And whanne syr launcelot sawe he myghte not ryde vp in to the montayne <br> he there alyghte vnder an Appel tree <br>{{...}}<br> And then he leid hym doune to slepe <br> And thenne hym '''thoughte''' there came an old man afore hym <br> the whiche sayd A launcelot of euylle feythe and poure byleue <br> wherfor is thy wille tourned soo lyghtely toward thy dedely synne|translation=And when Sir Lancelot saw that he could not ride up into the mountain, he [[alight]]ed under an apple tree {{...}} and then he lay down to sleep. And then '''it seemed to him''' [lit. ''him thought''] that an old man came before him who said: "Lancelot, of evil faith and poor belief, why is thy will turned so lightly towards thy deadly sin?"}}
<!--Prior quote:And than hym '''thought''' there com an olde man afore hym whych seyde, ‘A, Launcelot, of evill wycked fayth and poore beleve!’-->

=====Translations=====
{{trans-top|to seem, to appear}}{{multitrans|data=
