try.content:
{{also|TRY|trý}}
==Translingual==

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol}}

# {{ISO 639|3}}

==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|trī}}, {{IPA|en|/tɹaɪ/|[t͡ʃɹaɪ̯]|[tɹ̝̊aɪ̯]|[t̠͡ɹ̠̊˔aɪ̯]|[t̠͡ɹ̠̊˔ʷaɪ̯]}}
* {{IPA|en|/tɹɑj/|a=SSB}}
* {{IPA|en|/tɹaɪ/|a=CA}}
** {{IPA|en|/tɹʌɪ/|[tɹəi̯]|a=dialectal}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-to try.ogg|a=UK|text=to try}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-try.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-try.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=1}}
* {{hmp|en|tri|tri-}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|trien|t=to separate out, sift, choose, select, evaluate, try a legal case}}, from {{der|en|xno|trier}}, {{m|xno|triher}}, {{m|xno|triere|t=to divide, separate, choose, select, prove, determine, try a case}}, {{der|en|fro|trier|t=to choose, pick out or separate from others, sift, cull}}, of {{unc|en|nocap=1}} origin. Cognate with {{cog|oc|triar|t=to choose, sort, scrutinise, peel}}, {{cog|ca|triar|t=to pick, choose, decide}}. Suggested to be derived from {{der|en|la-vul|*trītāre|t=to crush, grind, trample, wear out}}, itself derived from {{cog|la-cla|trītus|t=rubbed, worn down, pulverised}}, the past participle of {{m|la|tero|terō, terere|t=to rub, wear down, trample}}, though this derivation is incompatible with the Occitan form. Additionally, the shift in meaning from "rub, crush, trample" to "pick out, choose, cull" is difficult to explain. One suggestion is that the semantic shift might have originated from a Latin phrase {{m|la||*granum terere}} {{gl|"to tread the corn (in threshing)"; compare {{cog|la|trītūra|t=rubbing, chafing, friction" also "threshing}}}}, which has a parallel in the modern {{m+|fr|trier le grain|t=to sort the grain}}. Alternatively, perhaps derived from {{der|en|la-vul|*trīāre}}, a metathetic alteration of {{m|la-vul|*tīrāre|t=to tear off, pull, draw}}, whence also {{der|en|fro|tirer|t=to draw, pull, pluck, tug, peck at, extract}}, {{cog|oc|tirar|t=to take, draw, retrieve, remove, extract}}.

Replaced native {{cog|enm|cunnen|t=to try}} (from {{cog|ang|cunnian}}), {{cog|enm|fandien|t=to try, prove}} (from {{cog|ang|fandian}}), and {{cog|enm|costnien|t=to try, tempt, test}} (from {{cog|ang|costnian}}).

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|en|trie||obsolete}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[attempt]]; to [[endeavour]]. Followed by infinitive.
#: {{ux|en|I '''tried''' to rollerblade, but I couldn’t.}}
#: {{ux|en|''Can you start the car? —I'll '''try''' (to)''.}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XLIV|page=361|passage=Not unnaturally, &ldquo;Auntie&rdquo; took this communication in bad part. Thus outraged, she showed herself to be a bold as well as a furious virago. Next day she found her way to their lodgings and '''tried''' to recover her ward by the hair of the head.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|year=1980|author=w:Leigh Brackett; et al|title=w:The Empire Strikes Back
|passage=''Skywalker:'' Alright... I'll give it a try.<br>''Yoda:'' NO! '''Try''' not! Do, or do not. There is no "'''try'''".}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2014-06-21|volume=411|issue=8892| title=Magician’s brain|url=http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21604535-real-sir-isaac-newton-was-not-first-king-reason-last| passage=[Isaac Newton] was obsessed with alchemy. He spent hours copying alchemical recipes and '''trying''' to replicate them in his laboratory. He believed that the Bible contained numerological codes.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To divide; to [[separate]].
## To [[separate]] (precious metal etc.) from the [[ore]] by melting; to [[purify]], [[refine]].
##* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1590|book=II|canto=VII|page=281|passage={{...}}euery feend his buſie paines applyde, / To melt the golden metall, ready to be '''tryde'''.}}
## {{lb|en|one sort from another}} To winnow; to sift; to pick out; frequently followed by ''out''.
