===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[exert]] for the sake of [[training]] or [[improvement]]; to [[practice]] in order to [[develop]].
#: {{ux|en|to '''exercise''' troops or horses;&emsp; to '''exercise''' one's brain with a puzzle}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To perform [[physical]] [[activity]] for [[health]] or training.
#: {{ux|en|I '''exercise''' at the gym every day.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To use (a right, an option, etc.); to [[put into practice]].
#: {{syn|en|assert}}
#: {{co|en|'''exercise''' caution}}
#: {{ux|en|The tenant '''exercised''' his option to renew the tenancy.}}
#: {{ux|en|She is going to '''exercise''' her right to vote.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Ezekiel|22|29|| The people of the land haue vsed oppression, and '''exercised''' robbery, and haue vexed the poore and needie: yea, they haue oppressed the stranger wrongfully.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1955|month=July|author=R. S. McNaught|title="Foreign" Engine Workings|journal=Railway Magazine|page=497|text=The pre-grouping railway managements were very chary about allowing other people's trains on their territory, and there were many instances where running powers granted by Act of Parliament were never actually '''excercised''' because of difficulties deliberately put in the way.}}
# {{lb|en|now|often|in passive}} To occupy the attention and effort of; to task; to tax, especially in a painful or vexatious manner; harass; to vex; to worry or make anxious.
#: {{ux|en|'''exercised''' with pain}}
#: {{ux|en|It is not a question that has '''exercised''' me greatly [[in the past]].}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=2|lines=88–89|passage=Where pain of unextinguiſhable fire / Muſt '''exerciſe''' us without hope of end|year=1873}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To set in action; to cause to act, move, or make exertion; to give employment to.
#* {{RQ:KJV|Acts|24|16|| And herein doe I '''exercise''' my selfe to haue alwayes a conscience void of offence toward God, and toward men.}}
#* {{RQ:Vance Outsider|page=373|column=2|passage=Little disappointed, then, she turned attention to "Chat of the Social World," gossip which '''exercised''' potent fascination upon the girl's intelligence.}}

