robot.content:
{{also|Appendix:Variations of "robot"}}
{{minitoc}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2006|December|17}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|a=RP|/ˈɹəʊ.bɒt/|/ˈɹəʊ.bət/<a:dated>}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-robot.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|a=GA|/ˈɹoʊ.bɑt/}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-robot.ogg|a=California}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹəʉ̯.bɔt/|a=AU}}
** {{audio|en|en-au-robot.ogg|a=Queensland}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɐʉ.bɔt/|[ˈɹɐ̞ʉ̯.bɔt]|a=NZ}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊbɒt|əʊbət|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|ro|bot}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃erbʰ-}}
{{dercat|en|zlw-ocs|sla-pro|ine-bsl-pro}}
From {{der|en|de|Robot}}, from a {{der|en|zlw}} language, ultimately related to Etymology 2, below.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|-}}

# {{lb|en|now|historical}} A system of [[serfdom]] used in Central Europe, under which a tenant's rent was paid in forced labour. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1849|journal=Littell's Living Age|volume=23|page=309
|passage=“I say again, down with the '''''robot'''''!—he is a dog who yields it!”}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=w:Tim Blanning|title=The Pursuit of Glory|page=159|publisher=Penguin|year_published=2008
|passage=Although the '''''robot''''' varied from region to region, it was rarely less than burdensome.}}

===Etymology 2===
[[File:Paris Motor Show 2018, Paris (1Y7A1752).jpg|thumb|An industrial '''robot''' ({{senseno|en|Q11012}}) engraving on a metal plate]]
{{etymid|en|R.U.R.}}
{{etymon|en|id=Q11012|:bor|cs:robot<id:Q11012>|tree=1}}

{{bor+|en|cs|robot}}, from {{m|cs|robota||drudgery, servitude}}. {{coin|cs|Karel Čapek|nat=Czech|occ=science-fiction writer and playwright|in=1920}} in the play ''{{w|R.U.R.}}'' after having been suggested to him by his brother {{w|Josef Čapek|Josef}}.<ref>{{cite-web|en|date=2021-03-24|last=Adams|first=Caralee|title=Major SciFi Discovery Hiding in Plain Sight at the Internet Archive|publisher=w:Internet Archive|work=Internet Archive Blogs|url=https://blog.archive.org/2021/03/24/major-scifi-discovery-hiding-in-plain-sight-at-the-internet-archive/}}</ref> Largely displaced {{ncog|en|automaton}}.

Ultimately a cognate with {{cognate|enm|arveth}} from {{cognate|ang|earfoþe}}, and {{cognate|de|Arbeit}}; see dialectal {{m|en|erf}}. {{dbt|en|#Etymology 1|alt1=''etymology 1''}}. Related to {{m+|en|orphan}}, but with a ''-t-'' suffix from {{der|en|sla-pro|-}} instead of the ''-an-'' from {{cog|grc|-}}.

Compare {{cog|pl|robotnik}} and {{cog|ru|работник||worker}}, sometimes mistakenly cited as the direct source of the English word.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|science fiction}} An [[intelligent]] [[mechanical]] [[being]] [[design]]ed to look like a [[human]] or other [[creature]], and usually made from [[metal]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Capek Selver RUR|page=17|passage=Young Rossum invented a worker with a minimum amount of requirements. He had to simplify him. He rejected everything that did not contribute directly to the progress of work—everything that makes man more expensive. In fact, he rejected man and made the '''Robot'''. My dear Miss Glory, the '''Robots''' are not people. Mechanically they are more perfect than we are, they have an enormously developed intelligence, but they have no soul.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2010-01-26|author=Tom Chivers; Iain McDiarmid|work=The Telegraph|passage=The '''robots''' in Dick's novel, loosely adapted by Ridley Scott into the film Blade Runner, were so similar to humans that when they went rogue, trained bounty hunters were called in to perform psychological tests to see whether suspected androids lacked human empathy.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q11012}} A [[machine]] built to carry out some [[complex]] [[task]] or group of tasks by physically [[move|moving]], especially one which can be [[programmed]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:robot}}
#: {{hyper|en|automaton}}
#: {{hypo|en|android}}
#: {{ux|en|We have a '''robot''' in the house that does the vacuuming.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=2010-05-16|author=Tim Webb|title=BP fights to stop the Gulf of Mexico spill – and salvage its reputation|passage=It's painfully slow and complex work which has never been attempted before in these conditions: the small box-shaped '''robots''', equipped with two claws, are operating in almost freezing water 5,000ft below the surface, in pitch black and strong currents.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210609221140/https://www.theguardian.com/business/2010/may/16/bp-gulf-of-mexico-oil-spill}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=John Liu|title=Elon Musk thinks robots are a $10 trillion business. He’s got some competition from China|url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/25/tech/china-robots-market-competitiveness-intl-hnk|date=2025-03-25|passage=Compared to humanoids, industrial '''robots''' typically feature less advanced technology and perform less sophisticated tasks. They’re widely used in industrial settings for manufacturing or transportation. {{...}} About 56% of the humanoid '''robot''' supply chain companies are based in China, according to a Morgan Stanley research report last month.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250731013813/https://www.cnn.com/2025/03/25/tech/china-robots-market-competitiveness-intl-hnk}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[bot]], software designed to perform a task.
#* {{RQ:WaPo|author=Lisa Bonos; Danielle Abril|title=No one likes meetings. They’re sending their AI note takers instead.|date=2025-07-02|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250702185410/https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2025/07/02/ai-note-takers-meetings-bots/|passage=Clifton Sellers attended a Zoom meeting last month where '''robots''' outnumbered humans.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively}} A [[person]] who does not seem to have any [[emotions]] or [[individuality]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=22 Dec 73|volume=1|number=27|journal=Gay Community News|page=3|author=Satya|title=It Is Not We Who Must Change|text=Straight society tries to change us by several means. Most of the time, it is mental torture, though physical abuse is not uncommon. We are programmed to be straight starting from the day we are born, and every action, word, and feeling must conform to the straight image. If we DO decide to be free rather than to be '''robots''', here are some of the consequences.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Murray N. Rothbard|title=Making Economic Sense|page=xiv|passage=Yet surely he was a humorless '''robot''' of a man, spewing forth lonely and bitter critiques of all those lesser mortals with whom he could not identify.|year=2006}}
# {{lb|en|South Africa}} A [[traffic light]] (from earlier {{m|en|robot policeman}}). {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
# {{lb|en|surveying}} A [[theodolite]] which follows the movements of a [[prism]] and can be used by a one-man [[crew]].
# {{lb|en|dance|preceded by definite article}} A style of [[dance]] popular in [[disco]] in which the dancer [[imitate]]s the stiff movements of a stereotypical [[android]] robot.

