===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂er-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|resoun}}, {{m|enm|reson}}, from {{der|en|xno|raisun}} ({{der|en|fro|raison}}), from {{der|en|la|ratiō}}, from {{m|la|ratus}}, past participle of {{m|la|reor||reckon}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂reh₁-||to think}}, reanalysed root of {{m|ine-pro|*h₂er-||to put together}}. Displaced {{l+|ang|racu||logic, reason}} and native {{cog|enm|-rede|reden|pos=found in compounds}}, from {{cog|ang|rǣden|t=condition, stipulation, calculation, direction}}, from the same Proto-Indo-European source (compare {{cog|fy|reden|t=reason}}, {{cog|nl|reden|t=reason}}). {{doublet|en|ration|ratio}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[cause]]:
#: {{syn|en|occasion}}
## That which [[cause]]s something: an [[efficient cause]], a [[proximate cause]].
##: {{ux|en|The '''reason''' this tree fell is that it had rotted.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author={{w|Daniel Dennett|Daniel Clement Dennett}}|title={{w|Darwin’s Dangerous Idea}}: Evolution and the Meanings of Life|page=198
|passage=There is a '''reason''' why so many should be symmetrical: The selective advantage in a symmetrical complex is enjoyed by all the subunits{{...}}}}
## A [[motive]] for an action or a determination.
##: {{ux|en|He made '''reason''' with his boss about taking a [[day off]].}}
##: {{ux|en|The '''reason''' I robbed the bank was that I needed the money.}}
##: {{ux|en|If you don't give me a '''reason''' to go with you, I won't.}}
##* '''1806''', Anonymous, Select Notes to Book XXI, in, {{w|Alexander Pope}}, translator, ''The {{w|Odyssey}} of {{w|Homer}}'', volume 6 (London, F.J. du Roveray), page 37:
##*: This is the '''reason''' why he proposes to offer a libation, to atone for the abuse of the day by their diversions.
##* {{RQ:James Portrait|chapter=10|passage=Ralph Touchett, for '''reasons''' best known to himself, had seen fit to say that Gilbert Osmond was not a good fellow{{...}}}}
## An [[excuse]]: a [[thought]] or a [[consideration]] offered in support of a determination or an opinion; that which is offered or accepted as an explanation.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1966|author=w:Graham Greene|title={{w|The Comedians (novel)|The Comedians}}|page=14|publisher=w:Penguin Classics|isbn=0140184945
|passage=I have forgotten the '''reason''' he gave for not travelling by air. I felt sure that it was not the correct '''reason''', and that he suffered from a heart trouble which he kept to himself.}}
## {{lb|en|logic}} A [[premise]] placed after its [[conclusion]].
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[rational|Rational]] [[think]]ing (or the [[capacity]] for it); the [[cognitive]] [[faculty|faculties]], collectively, of [[conception]], [[judgment]], [[deduction]] and [[intuition]].
#: {{ux|en|Mankind should develop '''reason''' above all other virtues.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|I|iii|page=132|passage=Were ſuch things here, as we doe ſpeake about? / Or haue we eaten on the inſane Root, / That takes the '''Reaſon''' Priſoner?}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=w:H.G. Wells|title=w:The War of the Worlds|publisher=William Heinemann|location=London|page=113|passage=The tremendous tragedy in which he had been involved - it was evident he was a fugitive from Weybridge - had driven him to the very verge of his '''reason'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1970|author=w:Hannah Arendt|title=On Violence|isbn=0156695006|page=62
|passage=And the specific distinction between man and beast is now, strictly speaking, no longer '''reason''' (the ''lumen naturale'' of the human animal) but science{{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2014-06-21|volume=411|issue=8892
|title=Magician’s brain
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/books-and-arts/21604535-real-sir-isaac-newton-was-not-first-king-reason-last
|passage=The [Isaac] Newton that emerges from the [unpublished] manuscripts is far from the popular image of a rational practitioner of cold and pure '''reason'''. The architect of modern science was himself not very modern. He was obsessed with alchemy.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Something [[reasonable]], in accordance with thought; [[justice]].
#* '''16th century''' {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''Lines on his Promised Pension''
#*: I was promised, on a time, To have '''reason''' for my rhyme.
# {{lb|en|mathematics|obsolete}} [[ratio|Ratio]]; [[proportion]].
#* {{RQ:Barrow Mathematical Learning|chapter=Lecture XVII. Of the Names and Diversities of the Twofold Kind of Reason or Proportion, viz. Arithmetical and Geometrical|pages=323–324|pageref=324|passage=[I]f two Quantities repreſented by the Numbers 20 and 4 be compared, by dividing the ''Antecedent'' 20 by the ''Conſequent'' 4, the ''Quotient'' is 5; but inverting the Terms, by dividing 4 by 20 the ''Quotient'' is <math>\tfrac{4}{20}=\tfrac{1}{5}</math>. By which ''Quotients'' are declared the ''Geometrical '''Reaſons''''' of the propoſed Quantities, becauſe if the ''Quotient'' found be multiplied by the ''Conſequent'', the ''Product'' is equal to the ''Antecedent''; for in the former Compariſon <math>5\times4=20</math>, in the latter <math>\tfrac{1}{5}\times20=4</math>; as Things again are referred to ''Equality''.}}

