==English==

===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:uder|fro:entreveue<ety:from<entreveoir>>|tree=1}}
From {{uder|en|fro|entreveue}} ({{cog|fr|entrevue}}), feminine singular past participle of {{m|fro|entrevëoir}}, from {{m|fro|entre-}} + {{m|fro|vëoir||to see}}. Equivalent to {{af|en|inter-|view}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪn.tə.vjuː/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪn.(t)ɚ.vju/|a=GA,CA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-interview.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɪn.tə.vjʉː/|a=Australia}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[official]] [[face-to-face]] meeting of [[monarch]]s or other important figures. {{defdate|16th|19th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|audience|}}
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition=2nd|part=II|section=2|member=4|passage=To be present at an '''interview''', as that famous of Henry the Eighth and Francis the First, so much renowned all over Europe{{nb...}}, no age ever saw the like.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1918|author=James George Frazer|title=Folk-Lore In The Old Testament|volume=1|page=215|text=In the ninth century of our era an Arab traveller {{...}} made his way by sea from Bassorah to India and thence to China. Arrived there, he sought an '''interview''' with the Chinese emperor, alleging as part of his credentials that he was of the family of the Prophet Muhammad.}}
# Any face-to-face [[meeting]], especially of an [[official]] or [[adversarial]] nature. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{Q|en|{{w|Gore Vidal}}|{{w|Burr (novel)|Burr}}|'', chapter 28''|year=1973|quote=''(Aaron Burr narrating):'' It was at about this time that I learned exactly what it was that Hamilton had said of me, and knew that this world was far too narrow a place to contain the two of us. Hamilton's friend made one further attempt to get him off the hook but only further impaled the slanderer by remarking that should Colonel Burr wish to enquire of any ''other'' conversation of Hamilton concerning Burr, a prompt and frank avowal or denial would be given. This was too much. I told Van Ness ''to set a time and place for an '''interview''''' [referring to a duel with pistols].}}
# A [[conversation]] in person (or, by extension, over the telephone, Internet etc.) between a [[journalist]] and someone whose opinion or statements he or she wishes to record for publication, broadcast etc. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The reporter gave the witness an '''interview'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|He has a job '''interview''' on Saturday.}}
# A [[formal]] [[meeting]], [[in person]], for the [[assessment]] of a [[candidate]] or [[applicant]]. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|It was a dreadful '''interview'''; I have no hope of getting the job.}}
# An [[audition]].
# A police [[interrogation]] of a [[suspect]] or party in an investigation. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}

