From {{inh|en|enm|smellen}}, {{m|enm|smillen}}, {{m|enm|smyllen}}, {{m|enm|smullen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|*smyllan}}, {{m|ang|*smiellan|t=to smell, emit fumes}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*smallijan|t=to glow, burn, smoulder}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*smel-|t=to burn, smoke, smoulder; tar, pitch}}. The noun is from {{inh|en|enm|smel}}, {{m|enm|smil}}, {{m|enm|smul||smell, odour}}. Related to {{cog|stq|smeele|t=to smoulder}}, {{cog|dum|smolen|smōlen|to burn, smoulder}} (whence {{cog|nl|smeulen||to smoulder}}), {{cog|gml|smölen||to be hazy, be dusty}} (whence {{cog|nds|smölen||smoulder}}), {{cog|nds|smullen||emit smoke}}, {{cog|vls|smoel||stuffy, muggy, hazy}}, {{cog|da|smul||dust, powder}}, {{cog|lt|smilkyti||to incense, fumigate}}, {{cog|lt|smilkti||to smudge, smolder, fume, reek}}, {{cog|lt|smalkinti||to fume}}, {{cog|mga|smál}}, {{m|mga|smól}}, {{m|mga|smúal||fire, gleed, embers, ashes}}, {{cog|ru|смола́||resin, tar}}. Compare {{l|en|smoulder}}, {{l|en|smother}}.

