===Etymology 1===
{{etymon|en|id=beast of burden|:inh|enm:camel}}
From {{inh|en|enm|camel}}, through {{inh|en|ang|camel}} and {{der|en|fro-nor|camel}} ({{cog|fro|chamel}}, modern {{cog|fr|chameau}}), from {{der|en|la|camēlus}}, from {{der|en|grc|κάμηλος}}, from a {{der|en|sem}} source, ultimately from {{der|en|sem-pro|*gamal-}}; compare {{cog|ar|جَمَل|id=camel}}, {{cog|he|sc=Hebr|גמל|tr=gamál}}, {{cog|arc|ܓܡܠܐ|tr=gamlā}}, {{cog|cop|ϭⲁⲙⲟⲩⲗ}}. As a marine device, from {{bor|en|nl|-}}. As an ethnic slur, short for {{m|en|camelfucker}}, {{m|en|camel jockey}}, etc.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q106695054}} A [[mammalian]] [[beast of burden]], much used in [[desert]] areas, of the genus {{taxfmt|Camelus|genus}}.
#: {{syn|en|oont|q1=India (Anglo-Indian), Australia, colloquial|ship of the desert}}
#: {{hyper|en|camelid|beast of burden|retromingent}}
#: {{hypo|en|Bactrian camel|deloul|dood|dromedarian|dromedary|hajeen|mehari}}
#: {{cot|en|bray|camlet|dromedarist|hajjan|hooshta|kajawah|mahmal|nose line|nose peg|sarwan|water bag|water cell|;|''see also'' [[:Category:en:Camelids|Category:en:Camelids]]}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Tamburlaine|part=1|scene=ii|page=23|passage=Returne our Mules and emptie '''Camels''' backe,<br>That we may trauell into ''Siria'', {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Richard 2 Q1|act=V|scene=v|sig=I3|verso=1|page=76|passage=It is as hard to come, as for a '''Cammell''' / To threed the poſterne of a ſmall needles eie: {{...}}|footer=A reference to [[w:Gospel of Matthew|Matthew]] 19:24, [[w:Gospel of Mark|Mark]] 10:25, and [[w:Gospel of Luke|Luke]] 18:25 in the [[Bible]].}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida|act=1|scene=2|passage=Achilles! a drayman, a porter, a very '''camel'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=The Explorer|passage=As sometimes happens in countries of small civilisation, a leader arose from among the Arabs. None knew from where he sprang, and it was said that he had been a '''camel''' driver.|year=1907|author=w:W. Somerset Maugham|chapter=IV|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/27198/pg27198-images.html}}
# A light [[brownish]] color, like that of a camel (also called camel brown).
#: {{color panel|C19A6B}}
# {{senseid|en|Q820997}}{{lb|en|typically|in the plural}} A [[vessel]] or [[tank]], [[typically]] [[paired]], [[use]]d to [[make]] an [[object]] [[more]] [[buoyant]].
#: {{syn|en|sea camel<id:Q820997>|caisson<id:Q820997>}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|pontoon<id:Q820997 or comparable>}}
#* '''1961''' April, H. Flint Ranney, "Whaling and Nantucket{{ndash}}The Decline: The Civil War, Petition to Congress, and the '''Camels'''", ''Historic Nantucket'', Vol. 8, No. 4, p. 59:
#*: Nantucket Island is probably the only place in the United States where '''camels''' were put to work to solve the problem created by a sandbar. They were Marine '''camels''', and like their live namesake, they were of a rugged construction and were designed to do very heavy work. In the shape of two large wooden boxes, the '''camels''' were flooded with sea water until they sank to a low level in the water; they were then arranged on each side of a heavily-loaded ship and drawn tightly together around the ship by means of heavy chains passing under the ship's hull. As the sea water was pumped out of the '''camels''', they rose up to a higher level, raising the ship between them, and when fully emptied the '''camels''' and the ship were towed across the bar easily and safely because of the lesser draft. First used in Holland as early as 1688, the '''camels''' were invented by one M. M. Bakker, who named them for their great strength. The Dutch used them to carry large ships over the [[Pampas]], which was a passage between two sandbanks in the [[Zyder Zee]], opposite the mouth of the River Y, and about six miles from the city of [[Amsterdam]]. The Russians adopted the idea and used '''camels''' for carrying ships over the shoals at [[Neva]]...
# {{lb|en|ethnic slur}} A person of Middle Eastern origin.
# {{lb|en|chess}} A [[fairy chess]] piece that is moved three squares in one direction and one at right angles to that direction in a single move, leaping over any intervening pieces.
#* {{quote-web|en|title=Simple Chess Variants|author=G. P. Jelliss|url=https://mayhematics.com/v/simplevariants.pdf|date=2010-08-21|page=9|work=Mayhematics|passage=The fers and '''camel''' can reach all the cells of one colour. The others are more restricted; the dummy cannot move at all, and the commuter can only move back and forth between two cells.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260310010159/http://www.mayhematics.com/v/simplevariants.pdf|archivedate=10 March 2026}}

