===Etymology 2===
{{wp|+ (body of water)}}
{{etymon|en|:bor|nl:kil<id:kill>|id=creek|tree=1}}
Borrowing from {{bor|en|nl|kil|id=kill|pos=formerly spelled {{m|nl|kill}}}}, from {{der|en|dum|kille}}, from {{der|en|odt|*killa}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*killjā}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*kiljǭ||fissure, slit; a type of stream, creek, channel}}. {{dbt|en|Kiel}}.

Most place names with '''''kill''''' were derived entirely in Dutch, but many have been subsequently {{lg|calqued}} into English or [[anglicize]]d by {{lg|folk etymology}}; for example, {{m|en|Motherkill}} and {{m|en|Murderkill}} both likely derive from {{m+|nl||*Modderkil|Mud Creek}} (better preserved in {{m|en|Mudder Kill}} and {{m|en|Muddy Kill}}), originally having nothing to do with mothership or murder.<!-- however, compare Moordener Kill -->

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}{{tlb|en|Northeast US|chiefly|New York}}

# {{topics|en|Landforms|Water}}{{lb|en|archaic|or|regional|largely {{lg|fossilized}} in place names}} A [[creek]] or [[stream]]; {{lb|en|more loosely}} any [[watercourse]], such as a [[channel]] or [[arm]] of the sea.
#: {{ux|en|The channel beyond Staten Island, which connects Newark Bay with Bergen Neck is the '''Kill''' van Kull, or the '''[[Kills]]'''.}}
## {{lb|en|preceded by ''[[the]]''}} In particular, a waterway with a name ending in '''''kill''''', such as the Beaverkill or the East Kill.
##: {{ux|en|We're gonna go flyfishing over in the '''kill'''.}}
{{rfquote|en}}

=====Usage notes=====
* No longer seems to be {{lg|productive}}, whether for deriving new {{lg|compound}}s or discussing [[waterway]]s not already called '''''Kill'''''. Almost exclusively used in proper names based on waterways, mainly [[tributary|tributaries]] of the [[Hudson]] and [[Delaware]] rivers and a few [[saltwater]] channels of [[New York City]]. The region in which they are found comprises southeastern [[New York State]] and adjacent parts of other states, i.e. former [[New Netherland]], itself roughly coinciding with [[Lenapehoking]] plus [[Mohican]] land, the area having been settled and (re)named by Dutch colonists in the 17th century prior to English acquisition. Compare the geographic term {{m|en|clove|id=valley}} for a similar case; {{m|en||hook}} (from {{m|nl|hoek}}) and {{m|en|-burg}} are also found as a second element of Dutch-origin [[toponym]]s.

