young.content:
{{also|Young}}
==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂ey-|id=life}}
Inherited from {{inh|en|enm|yong}}, {{m|enm|yonge}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ġeong}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*jung}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*jungaz}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*h₂yuHn̥ḱós}}, from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂yuh₁en-|t=young}}.
The modern pronunciation with {{IPAchar|/ʌ/}} reflects the regular shortening of Middle English {{IPAchar|/uː/}} (commonly represented by the letter sequence "ou") before velar consonants.
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|gsw|jung}}, {{m|gsw|jungu}}, {{m|gsw|junhs}}, {{m|gsw|jungà}}, {{m|gsw|jòng|t=young}}, {{cog|bar|junk|t=young}}, {{cog|gmw-cfr|-}}, {{cog|lb|jonk|t=young}}, {{cog|cim|djung}}, {{m|cim|jung}}, {{m|cim|junk|t=young}}, {{cog|nl|jong|t=young}}, {{cog|de|-}}, {{cog|nds-de|-}}, {{cog|mhn|-}} and {{cog|wym|jung|t=young}}, {{cog|li|jong}}, {{m|li|jonk|t=young}}, {{cog|yi|יונג|t=young}}, {{cog|da|-}}, {{cog|nb|-}}, {{cog|nn|-}} and {{cog|sv|ung|t=young}}, {{cog|fo|-}}, {{cog|is|ungur|t=young}}, {{cog|got|𐌾𐌿𐌲𐌲𐍃|t=young}}.
{{col-bottom}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/jʌŋ/}}
** {{audio|en|En-uk-young.ogg|a=RP}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-young.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/jʊŋɡ/|/jʊŋ/|a=Northern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌŋ|s=1}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|er}}

# In the [[early]] part of [[growth]] or [[life]]; [[born]] not long ago.
#: {{ux|en|a lamb is a '''young''' sheep}}
#: {{ux|en|these picture books are for '''young''' readers}}
#: {{ux|en|Come skydiving with us. Come on, you're only '''young''' once!}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1809-10-26|author=w:William Wordsworth|title=The French Revolution as It Appeared to Enthusiasts at Its Commencement|journal=Friend|section=No. 11, ll. 4-5
|passage=Bliss was it in that dawn to be alive,<br>But to be '''young''' was very heaven!}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|passage="What a charming amusement for '''young''' people this is, Mr. Darcy! There is nothing like dancing after all. I consider it as one of the first refinements of polished society."}}
#* {{RQ:Lincoln Pratt's Patients|chapter=1|passage=I stumbled along through the '''young''' pines and huckleberry bushes. Pretty soon I struck into a sort of path that, I cal'lated, might lead to the road I was hunting for. It twisted and turned, and, the first thing I knew, made a sudden bend around a bunch of bayberry scrub and opened out into a big clear space like a lawn.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Elizabeth Hess|title=Lost and Found
|passage=At Columbia-Greene, staffers go for the most unadoptable animals to save them from euthanasia. The '''youngest''', healthiest, cutest pets are waiting for you.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Arne Gerdner|title=Compulsory Treatment for Alcohol Use Disorders|page=85
|passage=The factors related to increase in number of abscondings were '''younger''' age, psychiatric problems, multi-drug abuse and living alone.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-07-19|author=[http://www.theguardian.com/profile/iansample Ian Sample]
|volume=189|issue=6|page=34|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=Irregular bedtimes may affect children's brains
|url=http://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2013/jul/08/irregular-bedtimes-affect-childrens-brains
|passage=Irregular bedtimes may disrupt healthy brain development in '''young''' children, according to a study of intelligence and sleeping habits.&nbsp; &para;&nbsp;Going to bed at a different time each night affected girls more than boys, but both fared worse on mental tasks than children who had a set bedtime, researchers found.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Jen Christensen|title=Young children are increasingly victims of opioid epidemic, study finds|url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/03/08/health/infant-opioid-deaths/index.html|date=2023-03-08|passage=The number of young children in the US who have died from opioid overdoses has increased significantly, according to a new study on accidental poisonings of children 5 and '''younger'''.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230921182814/https://www.cnn.com/2023/03/08/health/infant-opioid-deaths/index.html|archivedate=21 September 2023}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Libby Brooks and Severin Carrell|title=‘Hostile environment’ drives many of SNP’s female MSPs to step down before 2026 vote|url=https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/mar/23/snp-female-msps-women-stepping-down-elections-holyrood-scotland|date=2025-03-23|passage=These concerns were shared by other SNP women who told the Guardian there was a chronic lack of support from the party, including for those with '''younger''' families.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261027130721/https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2025/mar/23/snp-female-msps-women-stepping-down-elections-holyrood-scotland|archivedate=27 October 2026}}
# At an early stage of existence or development; having recently come into existence.
#: {{ux|en|the age of space travel is still '''young'''}}
#: {{ux|en|a '''young''' business}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Plague Year|page=23|passage={{...}} while the Fears of the People were '''young''', they were encreas’d strangely by several odd Accidents {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year=1975|author=w:David Bowie|title=Golden Years|album=Station to Station|passage=Look at that sky, life's begun / Nights are warm and the days are '''young'''}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023-08-24|author=Mairead Sheehy|journal=w:Irish Examiner|title=Irish group captures 'advanced image' of one of the youngest stars known to scientists|url=https://www.irishexaminer.com/news/arid-41211706.html|passage=A team of Irish astronomers have captured an out-of-this-world image of one of the '''youngest''' stars known to scientists.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251118175727/https://www.irishexaminer.com/news/arid-41211706.html|archivedate=18 November 2025}}
# (Not) advanced in age; (far towards or) at a specified stage of existence or age.
#* '''1906''', Robertson Nicoll, ''Tis Forty Years Since'', quoted in ''T. P.'s Weekly'', volume 8, page 462:
#*: And thou, our Mother, twice two centuries '''young''',
#*: Bend with bright shafts of truth thy bow fresh-strung.
#: {{ux|en|How '''young''' is your dog?}}
#: {{ux|en|Her grandmother turned 70 years '''young''' last month.}}
# [[junior|Junior]] (of two related people with the same name).
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1841|journal=The Museum of Foreign Literature, Science, and Art
|passage=The '''young''' Mr. Chester must be in the wrong, and the old Mr. Chester must be in the right.}}
# [[early|Early]] {{q|of a decade of life}}.
#* '''1922''', {{w|Elizabeth Louisa Moresby|E. Barrington}}, “The Mystery of Stella” in ''“The Ladies!” A Shining Constellation of Wit and Beauty'', Boston: Atlantic Monthly Press, pp. 40-41,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/007924887]</sup>
#*: {{...}} Miss Hessy is as pretty a girl as eye can see, in her '''young''' twenties and a bit of a fortune to boot.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1965|author=w:Muriel Spark|title=w:The Mandelbaum Gate|location=London|publisher=Macmillan|section=Part One, Chapter 1
|passage=Ephraim would be in his '''young''' thirties.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Alice Fisher|title=Grown-up chic is back as high street goes upmarket|date=20 January 2008|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2008/jan/20/fashion.lifeandhealth
|passage={{...}} while this may appeal to older, better-off shoppers, vast numbers, especially those in their teens and '''young''' twenties, still want fast, cheap fashion.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261025170620/https://www.theguardian.com/uk/2008/jan/20/fashion.lifeandhealth|archivedate=25 October 2026}}
# [[youthful|Youthful]]; having the [[look]] or [[quality|qualities]] of a young person.
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847
|title=Revenge of the nerds
|url=http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21582512-explosion-start-ups-changing-finance-better-revenge-nerds
|passage=Think of banking today and the image is of grey-suited men in towering skyscrapers. Its future, however, is being shaped in converted warehouses and funky offices in San Francisco, New York and London, where bright '''young''' things in jeans and T-shirts huddle around laptops, sipping lattes or munching on free food.}}
#: {{ux|en|My grandmother is a very active woman and is quite '''young''' for her age.}}
# Of or belonging to the early [[part]] of life.
#: {{ux|en|The cynical world soon shattered my '''young''' dreams.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Having little [[experience]]; [[inexperienced]]; [[unpracticed]]; [[ignorant]]; [[weak]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|I|1|text=Come, come, elder brother, you are too '''young''' in this.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|born not long ago}} {{l|en|youthful}}, {{l|en|junior}}; see also [[Thesaurus:young]]
* {{sense|having qualities of a young person}} {{l|en|youthful}}, {{l|en|juvenile}}
* {{sense|of or belonging to the early part of life}} {{l|en|juvenile}}
* {{sense|inexperienced}} {{l|en|underdeveloped}}, {{l|en|undeveloped}}, {{l|en|immature}}

====Antonyms====
* {{antsense|born not long ago}} {{l|en|old}}, {{l|en|aged}}, {{l|en|grown up}}, {{l|en|senior}}, {{l|en|youthless}}, {{l|en|elderly}}
* {{antsense|having qualities of a young person}} {{l|en|aged}}, {{l|en|old}}, {{l|en|youthless}}, {{l|en|mature}}, {{l|en|elderly}}
* {{antsense|of or belonging to the early part of life}} {{l|en|senior}}, {{l|en|mature}}, [[elderly]]
* {{antsense|inexperienced}} {{l|en|mature}}, {{l|en|experienced}}, {{l|en|veteran}}

====Derived terms====
