===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|wasten||to waste, lay waste}}, from {{der|en|xno|-}}, {{der|en|fro-nor|waster|t=to waste, devastate}} (compare also the variant {{m|fro|gaster}} and {{cog|fr|gâter}} from a related Old French word); the Anglo-Norman form ''waster'' was either from {{der|en|frk|*wōstijan|t=to waste}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*wāsto-|t=empty, wasted}}, or alternatively from {{der|en|la|vastō|vastāre}} and influenced by the Frankish; the English word was assisted by similarity to native {{cog|enm|westen}} {{gloss|"to waste"; > {{cog|en|weest}}}}. Cognate with {{cog|goh|wuostan}}, {{m|goh|wuastan}}, {{m|goh|wuostjan|t=to waste}} (Modern {{cog|de|wüsten}}), {{cog|ang|wēstan|t=to lay waste, ravage}}.

Displaced native {{noncog|ang|essian}} and {{m|ang|strȳndan}}. Largely overtook {{noncog|ang|forspillan}} and {{m|ang|wēstan}}.

