===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|trikke}}, from {{der|en|fro-nor|trique||}} (related to {{cog|fro|trichier|t=to defraud, act dishonestly, conceal, deceive, cheat}}; > modern {{cog|fr|tricher}}), itself possibly from {{der|en|gmh|trechen||to launch a shot at, play a trick on}}, or one of its derivatives (e.g. {{m+|gmh|ūftrechen|t=to do something to someone, hurt someone}}, {{m|gmh|vertrechen|t=to conceal, get over on someone}}, {{m|gmh|zuotrechen|t=to obtain falsely or deceitfully, wangle, finagle}}, etc.); yet the Old French verb is equally likely to be derived from {{der|en|la-vul|*triccāre}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|tricāre}}, from {{der|en|la|tricor|trīcor, trīcārī|dodge, search for detours; haggle, quibble}}.

The term has been connected to {{der|en|dum|treck}}, {{m|dum|trec|t=draw, line, desire, game move, cord, stratagem, ruse,  trick}}, from {{der|en|dum|trekken}}, {{m|dum|treken|trēken|to pull, place, put, move}}, from {{cog|odt|*trekken}}, {{m|odt|*trekan||to move, drag}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*trakjaną}}, {{m|gem-pro|*trekaną||to drag, scrape, pull}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dreg-||to drag, scrape}}. If they are related, ''trick'' would be cognate with {{cog|nds|trekken}}, {{cog|gmh|trecken}}, {{m|gmh|trechen}}, {{cog|da|trække}}, and {{cog|ofs|trekka}}, {{cog|ro|truc}} and other Romance languages.

Compare {{l|en|track}}, {{l|en|treachery}}, {{l|en|trig}}, and {{l|en|trigger}}.

