===Etymology 2===
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|cough|t=a cough; illness causing coughing}}{{nb...|caughe, cogh, coghe, coght, cohw, coughe, couhe, couth, couwe, couȝe, couȝhe, cowh, cowȝe, coȝgth, kogh, koghe, koghwhe, kouhe, kouȝh, kouȝhe, kouȝwhe, kuowh, kowh|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|pos=n|id=MED9969}}</ref> from {{m|enm|coughen|pos=verb}}: see [[#Etymology 1|etymology 1]].<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=7055670024|date=July 2023|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

The {{glossary|interjection}} is probably derived from the noun.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
{{examples|title=Example|example={{audio|en|Woman coughing three times.wav}}<br>A series of three coughs ''(noun {{senseno|en|cough n}})''.}}

# {{senseid|en|cough n}} A [[sudden#Adjective|sudden]], often [[involuntary]] [[expulsion]] of [[air#Noun|air]] from the [[lung]]s through the [[glottis]] ([[cause#Verb|causing]] a [[short#Adjective|short]], [[explosive#Adjective|explosive]] [[sound#Noun|sound]]), and out through the [[mouth#Noun|mouth]].
#: {{ux|en|Behind me, I heard a distinct, dry '''cough'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Parkinson Theatrum Botanicum|chapter=''Cyperus dulcis rotundus esculentus, Trasi dulce vocatus.'' The Most Delicate Sweet Cyperus, or Rush Nut.|page=148|passage=[I]t conduceth helpe to the crudities, humidities, and vvindineſſe of the ſtomacke and belly, and to helpe the ſhortneſſe of breath and '''coughes''': {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Works|volume=I|title=January and May|page=223|passage=The lumpiſh husband ſnoar'd avvay the night, / Till '''coughs''' avvak'd him near the morning light.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Canongate 2|volume=III|chapter=I|page=12|passage=Dwining ventured to give a low '''cough''' once or twice, by way of signal; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=V|page=26|passage=One saint's day in mid-term a certain newly appointed suffragan-bishop came to the school chapel, and there preached on “The Inner Life.”  He at once secured attention by his informal method, and when presently the coughing of Jarvis and another boy interrupted the sermon, he altogether captivated his audience with a remark about '''cough''' lozenges being cheap and easily procurable.|footer=An attributive use.}}
#* {{RQ:Wodehouse Inimitable Jeeves|page=139|passage=Jeeves coughed one soft, low, gentle '''cough''' like a sheep with a blade of grass stuck in its throat, and then stood gazing serenely at the landscape.}}
# A [[bout#Noun|bout]] of [[repeated#Adjective|repeated]] [[cough#Verb|coughing]] ''(verb {{senseno|en|cough intr}})''; also, a [[medical#Adjective|medical]] [[condition#Noun|condition]] that causes one to cough.
#: {{sense|medical condition}} {{synonyms|en|tussis}}
#: {{collocation|en|[[whooping cough|whooping '''cough''']]}}
#: {{ux|en|Sorry, I can’t come to work today—I’ve got a nasty '''cough'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|act=III|scene=ii|page=52|passage=[[w:Falstaff|[''John''] ''Fal''&#91;''staff''&#93;]]. VVhat diſeaſe haſt thou? / [''Peter''] ''Bul''[''lcaff'']. A horſon cold ſir, a '''cough''' ſir, vvhich I cought vvith ringing in the Kings affaires vpon his coronation day ſir.}}
#* {{RQ:Stowe Uncle Tom's Cabin|volume=II|chapter=Foreshadowings|page=81|passage='''Cough'''! you don't need to tell ''me'' about a '''cough'''. I've always been subject to a '''cough''', all my days. {{...}} O! Eva's '''cough''' is not anything.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A [[noise#Noun|noise]] or [[sound#Noun|sound]] like a cough ''({{senseno|en|cough n}})''.
# A [[vocalisation]] from a [[bird]] or other [[animal]] resembling a [[human]] cough.

