===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[occur]] or [[take place]].
#: {{syn|en|come to pass|Thesaurus:happen}}
#: {{ux|en|Let me tell you how it '''happened'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I hear there're fireworks '''happening''' tonight.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-28|author=w:Joris Luyendijk
|volume=189|issue=3|page=21|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=Our banks are out of control
|url=http://www.guardian.co.uk/commentisfree/joris-luyendijk-banking-blog/2013/jun/19/banking-britain-beyond-control
|passage=Seeing the British establishment struggle with the financial sector is like watching an alcoholic who still resists the idea that something drastic needs to '''happen''' for him to turn his life around.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To happen to; to [[befall]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=Sheridan Le Fanu|title=w:The House by the Churchyard|passage='By my soul! I believe something bad has '''happened''' me,' he muttered, and popped up his window, and looked out, half dreaming over the church-yard on the park beyond {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|_|or|_|impersonal|with infinitive}} To do or occur by [[chance]] or unexpectedly.
#: {{ux|en|Take an umbrella in case it '''happens''' to rain.}}
#: {{ux|en|Do you '''happen''' to have an umbrella?}}
#: {{ux|en|If you should ('''happen''' to) need any help, I'll be there.}}
#: {{ux|en|It '''happen'''ed that a [[police car]] was passing my house just at that moment.}}
# {{lb|en|followed by|_|{{m|en|on}} or {{m|en|upon}}}} To [[encounter]] [[by chance]].
#* {{RQ:Hawthorne Marble Faun|chapter=30
|passage=Unexpectedly, in a nook close by the farmhouse, he '''happened upon''' a spot where the vintage had actually commenced.}}
# {{lb|en|informal}} To become popular or trendy.

====Usage notes====
* In the sense which indicates a chance occurrence, ''happen'' is a [[catenative]] verb that takes the ''to''-infinitive. See [[Appendix:English catenative verbs]]

