===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|blok||log, stump, solid piece}}, from {{der|en|fro|bloc||log, block}}, from {{der|en|dum|blok||treetrunk}}, from {{der|en|odt|*blok||log}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*blokk}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*blukką||beam, log}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*bʰelǵ-||thick plank, beam, pile, prop}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|blok}}, {{cog|osx|blok}}, {{cog|goh|bloh}}, {{m|goh|bloc||block}}, {{cog|ang|bolca||gangway of a ship, plank}}, {{cog|non|bǫlkr||divider, partition}}. More at {{l|en|balk}}. See also {{l|en|bloc}}, {{l|en|bulk}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}
{{wp|Unicode +}}

{{Anchor|cuboid}}
# A substantial, often approximately [[cuboid]], piece of any substance.
#: {{co|en|a '''block''' of ice; a '''block''' of stone}}
#: {{ux|en| She picked up the '''block''' and examined it.}}
## A chopping block: a cuboid base for cutting or beheading.
##: {{ux|en|Anne Boleyn placed her head on the '''block''' and awaited her execution.}}
##* {{RQ:Orwell Animal Farm|1
|passage=You young porkers who are sitting in front of me, every one of you will scream your lives out at the '''block''' within a year.}}
## A [[wig block]]: a simplified head model upon which wigs are worn.
##* {{RQ:Melville Moby-Dick|[[s:Moby-Dick (1851) US edition/Chapter 13|chapter 13]]|text=Next morning, Monday, after disposing of the embalmed head to a barber, for a '''block''', I settled my own and comrade’s bill; using, however, my comrade’s money.}}
## A [[mould]] on which hats, bonnets, etc., are shaped.
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Much Ado About Nothing|I|i|passage=He wears his faith but as the fashion of his hat; it ever changes with the next '''block'''.}}
## {{lb|en|printing|dated}} A piece of hard wood on which a [[stereotype]] or [[electrotype]] [[plate]] is mounted.
## A [[case]] or [[frame]] housing one or more [[sheave]]s ([[pulley]]s), used with [[rope]]s to increase or redirect force, for example as part of lifting gear or a sailing ship's [[rigging]]. ''See also'' '''''[[block and tackle]]'''''.
##: {{syn|en|pulley block}}
## A section of split [[log]]s used as [[fuel]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1833|title=The Gospel Anchor|volume=2|page=371
|passage=She said, 'I hope I shall not be left to kill myself, but It would be no more sin to kill me, than to put a '''block''' on the fire.'}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1803|author=Mary Tighe|title=Selena|passage="Aye," said the farmer putting another '''block''' on the fire as he spoke {{...}}}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Ron Herrett|title=Shorty's Story
|passage=Dawn and Shorty would cut this tree into '''blocks''', while Randy and Matt went back for more. Dawn and Shorty made a good team on the crosscut, so when another log arrived, the first was almost completely made into shake wood.}}
## A set of sheets (of paper) joined together at one end, forming a cuboid shape.
##: {{co|en|a '''block''' of 100 tickets}}
## {{lb|en|falconry}} The [[perch]] on which a [[bird of prey]] is kept.
## {{lb|en|cellular automata}} In Conway's [[Game of Life]], a [[still life]] consisting of four living [[cell]]s arranged in a two-by-two square.
##* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1989-10-31|author=Scott Huddleston|title=life: glider guns|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/JTsjMQg9GZ0/m/_peJYMAtWKQJ|text=But there are many queen bee configurations in which the debris is neutralized, including placement of a '''block''' or eater near the bee's turnaround point, or placing two queen bees in a line or at right angles in various positions and phases.}}
##* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=1997-11-30|author=David Bell|title=Day & Night - An Interesting Variant of Life (part 5/5)|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/6Dy3GkgNN8g/m/YGlA3fMjWR0J|text=Perhaps the simplest puffer known is the following period 20 puffer based on the period 20 spaceship, which creates '''blocks'''.}}
##* {{quote-newsgroup|en|date=2005-02-23|author=Dave Greene|title=exist glider gun able of reconstruction in Life?|newsgroup=comp.theory.cell-automata|url=https://groups.google.com/g/comp.theory.cell-automata/c/Iw1hF3RcL9Q/m/VK6NuaMDRc0J|text='''Blocks''' have a couple of unusual properties: they are cleanly destroyed by an incoming glider on any one of six adjacent paths, and none of the possible collisions include any output gliders, so you can't get chain reactions.}}
# A physical area or extent of something, often rectangular or approximately rectangular.
#: {{co|en|a '''block''' of text; a '''block''' of colour; a '''block''' of land}}
## {{lb|en|philately}} A joined group of four (or in some cases nine) postage [[stamp]]s, forming a roughly square shape.
## {{lb|en|viticulture}} A discrete group of vines in a vineyard, often distinguished from others by variety, clone, canopy training method, irrigation infrastructure, or some combination thereof.
# A logical extent or region; a grouping or apportionment of like things treated together as a unit.
#: {{co|en|a '''block''' of data; a '''block''' of seven days; a '''block''' reservation}}
#* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=September 4, 2007|author=John Berg|title=Time on the Greens Beats Time at the Gate|url=http://www.nytimes.com/2007/09/04/business/04flier.html
|passage=The great game of golf offered an antidote to the inevitable dead space — '''blocks''' of difficult-to-use hours, pre- and postflight — that are one of business travel’s biggest drags.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20210110041622/https://www.nytimes.com/2007/09/04/business/04flier.html}}
## {{lb|en|computing}} A logical data storage unit containing one or more physical [[sector]]s.
##: {{cot|en|cluster}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=Dilip M. Ranade|title=Shared Data Clusters|page=79
|passage=After one disk is mapped, the next '''block''' starts at address 0 on the next disk.}}
##* {{RQ:Wired|date=2011-11-23|author={{w|Benjamin Wallace (writer)|Benjamin Wallace}}|title=The Rise and Fall of Bitcoin|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250102003010/https://www.wired.com/2011/11/mf-bitcoin/|passage=The difficulty of each puzzle would increase as the number of miners increased, which would keep production to one '''block''' of transactions roughly every 10 minutes. In addition, the size of each '''block''' bounty would halve every 210,000 '''blocks'''—first from 50 bitcoins to 25, then from 25 to 12.5, and so on. Around the year 2140, the currency would reach its preordained limit of 21 million bitcoins.}}
## {{lb|en|programming}} A region of [[code]] in a program that acts as a single unit, such as a [[function]] or [[loop]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=Matthew MacDonald|title=Beginning ASP.NET 2.0 in C# 2005: From Novice to Professional|page=49
|passage=With a foreach '''block''', you don't need to create an explicit counter variable.}}
## {{lb|en|cryptography}} A fixed-length group of [[bit]]s making up part of a [[message]].
## {{lb|en|chemistry}} A portion of a [[macromolecule]], comprising many [[unit]]s, that has at least one [[feature]] not present in adjacent portions.
## {{lb|en|rail}} A section of a [[railroad]] where the [[block system]] is used.
## {{lb|en|computing}} A [[contiguous]] [[range]] of [[Unicode]] [[code point]]s used to [[encode]] [[character]]s of a specific type; can be of any size evenly divisible by 16, up to 65,536 (a full [[plane]]).
##: {{ux|en|The "Specials" '''block''' comprises the sixteen codepoints from U+FFF0 through U+FFFF.}}
## {{lb|en|education}} A [[yeargroup]] at {{w|Eton College}}.
# A contiguous group of urban lots of property, typically several acres in extent, not crossed by public streets.
#: {{ux|en|I’m going for a walk around the '''block'''.}}
## The distance from one street to another in a [[city]] or [[suburb]] that is built (approximately) to a [[grid]] pattern.
##: {{syn|en|city block}}
##: {{ux|en|The place you are looking for is two long '''blocks''' east and one short '''block''' north.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1832| title=Records and Briefs of the United States Supreme Court |author=| page=423| passage=The Witness: Well, I have one that is 8/10 of a mile away; I have one that is just about another 8/10 of a mile away; I have one that is three '''blocks''' away; I have one that is four '''blocks''' away; I have one that is eight '''blocks''' away, and I have one that is about 14 '''blocks''' away.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2009|author=Hape Kerkeling|title=I'm Off Then|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=5rMpjPcdK9sC&pg=PT241|page=241|passage=This uphill trail is like a battlefield.   Anne offers to carry the older woman's backpack.  {{...}} I couldn't walk three '''blocks''' with the Danish woman's bulging backpack.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=2017| title=The Central Business District|author=Raymond E. Murphy| page=37| passage=A county courthouse and a municipal building, located approximately a city '''block''' north of the district, were automatically excluded because they were separated from the main CBD by several '''blocks''' that did not reach either of the required index values.}}
# A cuboid or approximately cuboid building.
#: {{co|en|a '''block''' of flats; a tower '''block'''; an office '''block'''; a toilet '''block'''; a shower '''block'''}}
#* {{RQ:Allingham China Governess|chapter=Foreword|passage=He turned back to the scene before him and the enormous new '''block''' of council dwellings. The design was some way after Corbusier but the '''block''' was built up on plinths and resembled an Atlantic liner swimming diagonally across the site.}}
## A [[cellblock]].
# Something that [[prevent]]s something from [[passing]].
#: {{synonyms|en|barrier|blockage|obstruction}}
#: {{ux|en|There’s a '''block''' in the pipe that means the water can’t get through.}}
## Interference or [[obstruction]] of [[cognitive]] processes.
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=01 Apr 77|journal=Gay Community News|page=14|title=COUNSELING (personal advertisement)|text=I work with writers/artists/others using artistic skills as a tool to explore '''blocks''' and free creative energy.}}
##: {{co|en|a [[mental]] '''block'''}}
##: {{co|en|[[writer's block|writer’s '''block''']]}}
## {{lb|en|backgammon}} Any [[point]] on the board where two or more men rest, and consequently an [[opponent]] may not land.
## {{lb|en|sports}} An action to interfere with the movement of an opposing player or of the object of play (ball, puck).
##: {{syn|en|stuff|roof|wall}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=February 12, 2011|author=Oliver Brett| title=Sunderland 1–2 Tottenham| work=BBC| url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9392691.stm| passage=The match proved an unedifying spectacle until Spurs won a corner following their first move of real quality, John Mensah making an important '''block''' with Jermain Defoe poised to strike.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20251112020812/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9392691.stm|archivedate=12 November 2025}}
### {{lb|en|cricket}} A [[shot]] played by holding the [[bat]] [[vertically]] in the [[path]] of the [[ball]], so that it loses [[momentum]] and drops to the [[ground]].
### {{lb|en|cricket}} The position of a player or bat when guarding the [[wicket]].
### {{lb|en|cricket}} A [[blockhole]].
### {{lb|en|cricket}} The [[popping crease]].
### {{lb|en|volleyball}} A defensive play by one or more players meant to deflect a spiked ball back to the [[hitter]]’s court.
### {{lb|en|gymnastics}} The portion of the movement where a [[gymnast]] pushes off the [[vault]].
###* {{RQ:en:New York Times|date=2024-07-27|author=Maggie Astor|title=Your Guide to Olympic Gymnastics: Vault|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/27/world/olympics/gymnastics-vault.html
|passage=While it might seem like she should bend her elbows in order to spring up — the way you bend your knees in order to jump — a good '''block''' actually requires the gymnast to keep her arms straight and use her shoulder strength to launch into the air.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20240731002605/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/07/27/world/olympics/gymnastics-vault.html}}
## {{sid|en|Q2125156, n}} {{lb|en|computing|social media}} A temporary or permanent [[ban]] that prevents access to an online account or service, or connection to or from a designated telephone number, IP address, or similar.
