#: {{ux|en|The user who started the edit war was '''blocked''' for a day to cool off.}}
#* {{RQ:Atlantic|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2024/03/dna-tests-incest/677791/|title=DNA Tests Are Uncovering the True Prevalence of Incest|date=2024-03-18|author=Sarah Zhang|passage=He messages her occasionally on Facebook, sending photos of grandkids and puppies he’s raised. Every year, he wishes her a happy birthday. She has not replied, but she has also not '''blocked''' him.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260731180313/https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2024/03/dna-tests-incest/677791/|archivedate=31 July 2026}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[bar]] (a message or communication), or bar connection with (an online account or service, a designated telephone number, IP address, etc.).
#: {{ux|en|They’ve '''blocked''' all calls to international numbers.}}
#: {{ux|en|Most Internet services have been '''blocked'''.}}
# {{lb|en|programming|intransitive}} To [[wait]] for some [[condition]] to become true.
#: {{ux|en|When the condition expression is false, the thread '''blocks''' on the condition variable.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Richard Blewett; Andrew Clymer|title=Pro Asynchronous Programming with .NET|page=25
|passage=Post is a “fire and forget” where the UI thread work is performed asynchronously; Send is synchronous in that the call '''blocks''' until the UI thread work has been performed.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[shape]], [[stretch]], or [[mould]] knitted items, hats, books (and book covers), shoes, etc.
#: {{ux|en|I '''blocked''' the mittens by wetting them and pinning them to a shaped piece of cardboard.}}
