*** {{audio|en|en-us-camp.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|æmp|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|camp}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kh₂em-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|kampe||battlefield, open space}}, from {{inh|en|ang|camp||battle, contest, battlefield, open space}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kamp||open field where military exercises are held, level plain}}, from {{der|en|la|campus||open field, level plain}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*kh₂em-|*kh₂emp-|t=to bend; crooked}}. Reinforced circa 1520 by {{der|en|frm|[[can]], [[camp]]||place where an army lodges temporarily}}, from {{der|en|fro-nor|camp}}, from the same {{der|en|la|-}} (whence also {{cog|fr|champ}} from Old French). Cognate with {{cog|goh|champf||battle, struggle}} ({{cog|de|Kampf}}), {{cog|non|kapp||battle}}, {{cog|goh|hamf||paralysed, maimed, mutilated}}. {{doublet|en|campus|champ}}.

The verb is from {{inh|en|enm|campen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|[[campian]], [[compian]]||to fight, war against}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kampōn||to fight, do battle}}, from {{m|gmw-pro|*kamp||field, battlefield, battle}}, see above. Cognate with {{cog|nl|kampen}}, {{cog|de|kämpfen||to struggle}}, {{cog|da|kæmpe}}, {{cog|sv|kämpa}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|outdoor place for temporary accommodation}} An [[outdoor]] place acting as temporary [[accommodation]] in tents or other simple structures.
#: {{hypo|en|base camp#Noun}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|campsite#Noun|campground#Noun|encampment#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|There's a big '''camp''' in the woods northwest of here. It's popular with hikers and bushcrafters.}}
# {{senseid|en|organized event taking place in outdoorsy accommodations}} An organised event, often taking place in tents or temporary accommodation.
#: {{hypo|en|summer camp#Noun|band camp#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|I met my best friend last summer at '''camp'''. She lives in Albany and plays the clarinet.}}
#: {{ux|en| He used to go to [[summer camp|summer '''camp''']] every alternate year.}}
#: {{ux|en|I met my girlfriend last summer at '''camp'''. You wouldn't know her — she lives in Canada.}}
# {{senseid|en|type of military base; instance thereof}} A base of a military group, not necessarily temporary.
#: {{nearsyn|en|encampment#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|Signs of enemy recon were found near their '''camp''' last week.}}
# {{senseid|en|place of politically motivated confinement in outdoorsy conditions}} A place of politically motivated [[confinement]] in outdoorsy conditions, usually also leading to [[slave labor]] and [[death]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|concentration camp#Noun|labor camp#Noun|reeducation camp#Noun|gulag#Noun|stalag#Noun|prison camp#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|Some say they'll round us all up into '''camps''', but if they come for us, we'll take some out with us, on our way out.}}
#* {{quote-web
|en
|date=06/18/2013
|author=Esther Felden
|title=Hell on earth
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20150622015840/https://www.dw.com/en/tortured-beaten-starved-life-in-a-north-korean-gulag/a-16889451
|archivedate=22 June 2015
|url=https://www.dw.com/en/tortured-beaten-starved-life-in-a-north-korean-gulag/a-16889451
|work=w:Deutsche Welle
|text=Mr. Ahn Myong-Chol was a prison guard at '''Camp''' 22 in Hoeryong and a driver at the '''camps'''. He was there between 1990 and 1994. He is the one who reported that prisoners had been used for human experimentation inside the '''camps'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|single hut or shelter}} A single hut or shelter.
#: {{coi|en|a hunter’s '''camp'''}}
# {{senseid|en|company or body of persons encamped}} {{lb|en|collective}} The company or body of persons encamped.
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=9|passage=The '''camp''' broke up with the confusion of a flight.}}
# {{senseid|en|faction}} {{lb|en|collective}} A group of people with the same strong [[ideal]]s or political leanings.
#: {{cot|en|cult#Noun}}
#: {{nearsyn|en|faction#Noun|ingroup#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|She's in the '''camp''' that speaks as if all vaccines were poisons.}}
# {{senseid|en|army, obsoletely}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[army]].
