===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ten-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|tente||}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|tente|}}, from {{der|en|la-vul|*tenta||tent}}, from the feminine of {{der|en|la|tentus||}}, ptp. of {{m|la|tendere||to stretch, extend}}, or contracted from {{m|la||*tendita}} as an alternate past participle. Displaced native {{cog|enm|tilt|tild, tilt|tent, tilt}}, from {{cog|ang|teld||tent}}. Compare {{cog|es|tienda||store, shop; tent}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q170544}}A [[pavilion]] or portable [[lodge]] consisting of skins, [[canvas]], or some strong cloth, stretched and sustained by poles, used for sheltering people from the weather.
#: {{ux|en|We were camping in a three-man '''tent'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We bought a new '''tent''' that can be put up in five seconds, but it took about twenty minutes to take it down and pack it away.}}
# {{lb|en|archaic}} The representation of a tent used as a [[bearing]].
# {{lb|en|Scotland}} A portable [[pulpit]] set up outside to accommodate worshippers who cannot fit into a church.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1824|author=w:James Hogg|title=The Private Memoirs and Confessions of a Justified Sinner
|passage=A splendid '''tent''' was erected on the brae north of the town, and round that the countless congregation assembled.}}
# A [[trouser tent]]; a piece of [[fabric]], etc. protruding outward like a tent.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013|author=Nathan Lapointe|title=A Strange New World|passage={{...}} feeling his erection making a '''tent''' in his pants.}}

